::ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2-5x+6=0\). {
=A. \(x=2\) and \(x=3\) #Correct. The equation factors as \((x-2)(x-3)=0\), so the solutions are 2 and 3.
~B. \(x=-2\) and \(x=-3\) #These signs would come from \((x+2)(x+3)=0\), which does not match the original equation.
~C. \(x=1\) and \(x=6\) #These values multiply to 6 but add to 7, not 5.
~D. \(x=-1\) and \(x=-6\) #These values do not produce the correct middle term when factored.
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] What property justifies setting \(x-4=0\) and \(x+1=0\) after factoring \(x^2-3x-4=0\) as \((x-4)(x+1)=0\)? {
~A. Distributive property #The distributive property explains expansion, but not why each factor can be set equal to zero.
=B. Zero-product property #Correct. If a product equals zero, at least one factor must equal zero.
~C. Commutative property #The commutative property changes order, not the solving step.
~D. Additive identity property #The additive identity involves adding 0, not solving a factored equation.
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A ball's height is modeled by \(h(t)=-16t(t-2)\). When does the ball return to the ground after launch? {
~A. \(t=-16\) seconds #The coefficient affects height scale, not the zero after launch.
~B. \(t=0\) seconds #This is the launch time, not the return after launch.
=C. \(t=2\) seconds #Correct. The positive zero after \(t=0\) means the ball returns to the ground at 2 seconds.
~D. \(t=16\) seconds #This uses the coefficient as a time, which is not a zero of the model.
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \((x+7)(x-2)=0\). {
~A. \(x=7\) and \(x=-2\) #The signs are reversed from the factors.
~B. \(x=-7\) and \(x=-2\) #The factor \(x-2\) gives \(x=2\), not \(-2\).
~C. \(x=7\) and \(x=2\) #The factor \(x+7\) gives \(x=-7\), not 7.
=D. \(x=-7\) and \(x=2\) #Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.
}

::ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L05_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A rectangle has area 48 square units and side lengths \(x\) and \(x+2\). Which equation should be solved to find \(x\)? {
~A. \(x+x+2=48\) #This adds side lengths and would describe a partial perimeter relationship, not area.
=B. \(x(x+2)=48\) #Correct. Area is length times width, so the product of the side lengths must equal 48.
~C. \(2x+2(x+2)=48\) #This represents perimeter, not area.
~D. \(x^2+2=48\) #This misses the \(2x\) term from multiplying \(x(x+2)\).
}
